## Actual adaptations (as of this pass — update when more occur)

- **`skills/systematic-debugging/SKILL.md`** — the hypothesis-test-
  minimal-fix-regression procedure structure is adapted from
  obra/superpowers' systematic-debugging skill (MIT, confirmed
  2026-08-11). No verbatim text was copied; the procedure was
  re-expressed against AIECP's own Evidence Model
  (`docs/evidence-model.md`) and workflow states
  (`workflows/bug-report.sm.yaml`). Attribution recorded here per MIT
  license terms and `constitution/constitution.md` §6 (Reuse before
  reinvent).

  **Deepening pass (2026-08-14).** The skill was enriched in place on
  branch `feature/a1-systematic-debugging-deepen` with practical
  detail re-expressed (not copied verbatim) from the same upstream
  skill. Source commit pinned for attribution:
  `obra/superpowers@b36e0829c6d0140e93cfef2ca599b1b07d4a7797`
  (HEAD of `main` at clone time; the last commit touching the
  `skills/systematic-debugging/` directory was
  `c8921b5156562b61af253150a4d1d01a71705be8`). The frontmatter and
  section headings of the skill file were preserved; the following
  additions were made by re-expressing upstream techniques in AIECP's
  own vocabulary (Evidence Model entities, workflow states):

  - **"When to use this skill"** — added a paragraph on
    pressure-resistance (why this skill matters most under time
    pressure, not least) and a list of internal anti-pattern moves that
    should trigger a return to Phase 1. Adapted from the upstream
    skill's "Red Flags" and "Common Rationalizations" sections; no
    sentence was copied.
  - **"1. Locate evidence"** — added concrete shell-level
    evidence-gathering commands (`git log`, `git diff`, `gh run view`,
    `git status`) and a multi-component boundary-instrumentation
    pattern with a 4-layer shell probe example. Re-expressed from the
    upstream skill's multi-layer diagnostic example, retargeted to
    emit AIECP `Event`/`Trace` artifacts rather than free-form logs.
  - **"2. Reproduce"** — added the deterministic-vs-probabilistic
    reproduction distinction and the condition-based-waiting technique
    (`waitFor(predicate)` with bounded timeout rather than fixed
    `sleep`). Adapted from the upstream skill's
    `condition-based-waiting.md` reference; re-expressed against the
    AIECP `Replay` requirement that a reproduction be replayable
    bit-for-bit.
  - **"3. Diagnose"** — added a backward-tracing-through-the-call-chain
    procedure with an instrumentation snippet for capturing
    `new Error().stack` before a failing operation, a "find polluter"
    bisection pattern for cross-test state pollution, and a
    three-failure rule that escalates from local hypotheses to
    architectural questioning after three rejected candidates.
    Adapted from the upstream skill's `root-cause-tracing.md`,
    `find-polluter.sh`, and the "Question Architecture" rule in Phase
    4 of the upstream procedure; all re-expressed against AIECP's
    `Decision`/`Validation`/`expected` entity model.
  - **"Tool integration"** — extended each tool's bullet with the
    concrete usage now expected of it (scriptable one-shot commands
    for `shell_exec`, prior-artifact reading for `filesystem_read`,
    bisect loop via `shell_exec` because `test_runner` does not
    naturally express per-file side-effect inspection).
  - **"Validation"** — added a "Defense in depth, not single-point fix"
    paragraph: when the root cause was invalid data propagation,
    guards must be added at every layer the value passes through (entry
    / business / environment / debug), each recorded as its own
    `Decision`. Adapted from the upstream skill's
    `defense-in-depth.md` reference; re-expressed against AIECP's
    `regression-protect` requirement that the deepest layer refuse bad
    input.
  - **"Examples"** — added a third worked example ("three-failure
    architectural case") demonstrating the three-failure rule firing
    and producing an architectural `Decision` rather than a fourth
    speculative patch.

  No file from the upstream repository was vendored; no script from
  `skills/systematic-debugging/` in the upstream repo
  (e.g. `find-polluter.sh`, `condition-based-waiting-example.ts`) was
  copied. The bisection pattern is described in prose and emitted as a
  one-line `for` loop in the skill body, written from scratch for this
  skill's AIECP-targeted narrative.

